# Dependency Injection with Hilt

## Overview

This project uses **Hilt** for dependency injection. All dependencies must be properly injected, never instantiated manually.

Reference DI modules:
- [NetworkModule.kt](mdc:app/src/main/java/com/example/baseapp/core/di/NetworkModule.kt)
- [DatabaseModule.kt](mdc:app/src/main/java/com/example/baseapp/core/di/DatabaseModule.kt)
- [RepositoryModule.kt](mdc:app/src/main/java/com/example/baseapp/core/di/RepositoryModule.kt)

## General Rules

### 1. Never Use Manual Instantiation
❌ **BAD**:
```kotlin
class MyViewModel {
    private val useCase = MyUseCase()  // DON'T
    private val repository = MyRepositoryImpl()  // DON'T
}
```

✅ **GOOD**:
```kotlin
@HiltViewModel
class MyViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val useCase: MyUseCase
) : ViewModel() {
    // Dependencies injected via constructor
}
```

### 2. Always Use Constructor Injection
```kotlin
class MyRepository @Inject constructor(
    private val remoteDataSource: RemoteDataSource,
    private val localDataSource: LocalDataSource
) : Repository {
    // Implementation
}
```

## Hilt Annotations

### Application Class
```kotlin
@HiltAndroidApp
class MyApplication : Application()
```

### Activities
```kotlin
@AndroidEntryPoint
class MainActivity : ComponentActivity()
```

### ViewModels
```kotlin
@HiltViewModel
class MyViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val useCase: MyUseCase
) : ViewModel()
```

### Composables (getting ViewModels)
```kotlin
@Composable
fun MyScreen() {
    val viewModel = hiltViewModel<MyViewModel>()
    // Use viewModel
}
```

## Module Structure

### 1. Network Module
```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
object NetworkModule {
    
    @Provides
    @Singleton
    fun provideRetrofit(): Retrofit {
        // Configuration
    }
    
    @Provides
    @Singleton
    fun provideApiService(retrofit: Retrofit): ApiService {
        return retrofit.create(ApiService::class.java)
    }
}
```

### 2. Database Module
```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
object DatabaseModule {
    
    @Provides
    @Singleton
    fun provideDatabase(@ApplicationContext context: Context): AppDatabase {
        return Room.databaseBuilder(
            context,
            AppDatabase::class.java,
            Constants.DATABASE_NAME
        ).build()
    }
    
    @Provides
    fun provideDao(database: AppDatabase): MyDao {
        return database.myDao()
    }
}
```

### 3. Repository Module
```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
abstract class RepositoryModule {
    
    @Binds
    @Singleton
    abstract fun bindMyRepository(
        impl: MyRepositoryImpl
    ): MyRepository
}
```

## Scoping

### Singleton Scope
Use for:
- Network clients
- Database instances
- Shared preferences
- Repository implementations

```kotlin
@Provides
@Singleton
fun provideApiService(): ApiService
```

### ViewModel Scope
Automatically provided for ViewModels:
```kotlin
@HiltViewModel
class MyViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val useCase: MyUseCase
) : ViewModel()
```

### Activity Scope
Use when needed:
```kotlin
@Provides
@ActivityScoped
fun provideActivitySpecificDependency(): Dependency
```

## Qualifiers

### When You Need Multiple Implementations
```kotlin
@Qualifier
@Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY)
annotation class IoDispatcher

@Qualifier
@Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY)
annotation class MainDispatcher

@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
object DispatcherModule {
    
    @Provides
    @IoDispatcher
    fun provideIoDispatcher(): CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.IO
    
    @Provides
    @MainDispatcher
    fun provideMainDispatcher(): CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.Main
}
```

Usage:
```kotlin
class MyRepository @Inject constructor(
    @IoDispatcher private val ioDispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher
)
```

## Testing with Hilt

### Test Modules
```kotlin
@Module
@TestInstallIn(
    components = [SingletonComponent::class],
    replaces = [NetworkModule::class]
)
object TestNetworkModule {
    @Provides
    @Singleton
    fun provideTestApiService(): ApiService {
        // Return mock implementation
    }
}
```

### Test Setup
```kotlin
@HiltAndroidTest
class MyScreenTest {
    
    @get:Rule(order = 0)
    val hiltRule = HiltAndroidRule(this)
    
    @get:Rule(order = 1)
    val composeTestRule = createAndroidComposeRule<MainActivity>()
    
    @Before
    fun setup() {
        hiltRule.inject()
    }
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Pattern 1: Use Case Injection
```kotlin
class MyUseCase @Inject constructor(
    private val repository: MyRepository
) {
    operator fun invoke(param: String): Flow<Result<Data>> {
        return repository.getData(param)
    }
}
```

### Pattern 2: Repository Injection
```kotlin
class MyRepositoryImpl @Inject constructor(
    private val remoteDataSource: RemoteDataSource,
    private val localDataSource: LocalDataSource,
    @IoDispatcher private val ioDispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher
) : MyRepository {
    // Implementation
}
```

### Pattern 3: ViewModel Injection
```kotlin
@HiltViewModel
class MyViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val getUsersUseCase: GetUsersUseCase,
    private val refreshUsersUseCase: RefreshUsersUseCase
) : ViewModel() {
    // Implementation
}
```

## Checklist for New Components

- [ ] Uses `@Inject constructor` for dependencies
- [ ] No manual instantiation of dependencies
- [ ] Appropriate Hilt annotation used (`@HiltViewModel`, `@AndroidEntryPoint`)
- [ ] Dependencies provided in appropriate module
- [ ] Correct scope applied (`@Singleton`, `@ViewModelScoped`)
- [ ] Qualifiers used when multiple implementations exist
- [ ] Test module created if needed for testing

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

❌ **DON'T**:
- Create instances with `new` or constructors
- Use service locator pattern
- Pass dependencies through multiple layers
- Forget `@AndroidEntryPoint` on Activity
- Forget `@HiltAndroidApp` on Application

✅ **DO**:
- Use constructor injection everywhere
- Define clear dependency graphs
- Use appropriate scopes
- Create test modules for testing
- Follow single responsibility principle